Z||000007||theogre||08-12-2001||03:27 PM||||I thought about doing this as a cave topic. It's come up a few times now. Here's the info I've got so far.<P>Car seats can be used in 99.something or other percent of all cars on the road. I'm sure many of them will work in Fiero seats.<P>Unfortunatly there is no simple answer to this. There are too many diferant infant/child car seats to be sure we give you the right data. To make it more confusing, the instructions that come with the seats are often dificult to follow.<P>To use many seats, you'll need the metal "clip" that ties the shoulder and lap straps together so you can put them thru the seat hardware.<P>You also have to make certain the inertial locks on the retractors work. If not, you need to fix that or add a non retractor belt like one person did above.<P>Also, before buying a seat, check <A HREF="http://http://www.nhtsa.gov/" TARGET=_blank>NHTSA (National Highway Transportation Safety Administration)</A> and <A HREF="http://http://www.cpsc.gov/" TARGET=_blank>CPSC (Consumer Product Safety Commission)</A> for seats under recall. many recall seats still end up on store shelves and "outlet store" or flea markets.<P>NOTE! Those of you that already have seats or strollers,,, check them on the above sites as well. There have been a bunch of seats and strollers recalled in the last year or so. The infant "buckets" that have the carry handle have been in a number of recalls. The handles fail and dump the baby.<P>Many cities and states are now offering "clinics" or instpections of seats to make sure they are used right. Some are available by appointment. Check with area police department public relations offices.<P>You should also contact the makers of diferant seats and see if they have any recomendations of models, methods, etc....<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000008||84Bill||08-12-2001||11:59 PM||||Got a baby seat in mine||||reg||1
Z||000009||annieL||08-13-2001||04:45 PM||||It is safe to have a childseat in the front as long as there is NO airbag.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||08-13-2001||07:35 PM||||Ideally you would not put the seat in front even if there is no air bag. The center of the rear seat is the safest place if you have the option. Either side of rear seat is next choice and front of non air bag car is last.<P>You would never put any child in the front seat with an air bag regardless of car seat or no. Just how old a kid is doesn't matter as much as how tall they are. Small kids need to stay away from airbags. <P>I had a page in process with a bunch of safety and standards links in the works. After thinking about this thread last night I finished it and posted it just now. It has links to NHTSA and CPSC that should help answer most child seat questions. <P>NHTSA even lists places you can go to have the installed seat checked to make sure it's put in right.<P>CPSC has all the recalls on car seats and strollers. Always check this list before buying a new or used seat/stroller. Many recalled products are still on store shelves.||||reg||1
